"Predicting Privileged Information Effectiveness for On-Policy Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1)\nciteturn41277view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1\",\"lineno\":292}); Total lines: 1041\nL266: Our PI ablations point the same way: the student’s own failed rollouts induce a larger $D$ than its successful ones, even though they make far worse PI (Appendix cite68†E ). Thus, shift direction and location matter in addition to magnitude.\nL267: Takeaway. A larger $D$ generally indicates a stronger PI training signal, but does not reveal whether the induced shift improves the policy.\nL268: ##### Error-step KL contrast consistently tracks post-training performance.\nL269: Across all nine student–benchmark combinations, $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ is positively correlated with post-training performance ($\\rho=0.10$–$0.94$). A larger contrast means that PI more strongly suppresses the student’s sampled tokens at judge-identified error steps than at comparable positions from the same task. Thus, PI formats that concentrate the distributional shift at critical mistakes tend to produce larger training gains.\nL270: This relationship is particularly strong for 4B-Instruct on AppWorld ($\\rho=0.94$) and 30B-Instruct on ALFWorld ($\\rho=0.92$). On $\\tau^{2}$, the contrast remains positively associated with performance for 4B-Thinking ($\\rho=0.70$), even though several trajectory formats have negative values. These small or negative contrasts and limited OPSD gains suggest poorly targeted supervision. This interpretation is consistent with cite124†Kaur et al.\nL271: [9] , who show that privileged distillation can penalize productive reconsideration tokens in thinking models: supervision may insufficiently suppress errors while disrupting useful behavior.\nL272: Takeaway. Effective PI concentrates token-level suppression on actual mistakes rather than comparable non-error steps, yielding a larger positive $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$.\nL273: ## 4 How Well Does OPSD Combine with GRPO?\nL274: \nL275: Figure 4: OPSD+GRPO performance on ALFWorld, AppWorld, and $\\tau^{2}$-retail. The top row shows performance across objective weights, and the bottom row shows training curves at $\\lambda=0.9$. Here, $\\lambda=0$ is vanilla OPSD and $\\lambda=1$ is GRPO; dashed lines show the matched GRPO baseline.\nL276: ### 4.1 Combined Training Objective\nL277: \nL278: Rather than optimizing over the many possible ways to combine token-level distillation and GRPO outcome rewards [cite125†21 ], we use a simple weighted objective to test whether PI identified as effective under vanilla OPSD remains effective when combined with GRPO. We use Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 for all experiments in this section. We combine the GRPO and OPSD signals at the advantage level:\nL279:  | $$A^{\\mathrm{OPSD+GRPO}}=\\lambda A^{\\mathrm{GRPO}}+(1-\\lambda)A^{\\mathrm{OPSD}},$$  |  | (7)\nL280: \nL281: where $A^{\\mathrm{GRPO}}$ is the outcome-based group-relative advantage, $A^{\\mathrm{OPSD}}$ is the token-level self-distillation advantage, and $\\lambda\\in[0,1]$ controls their relative weight.\nL282: \nL283: Figure cite126†4 compares four values of $\\lambda$ across three PI formats with matched GRPO-only controls on ALFWorld, AppWorld, and $\\tau^{2}$. The $\\lambda=0$ endpoint corresponds to vanilla OPSD.\nL284: ### 4.2 Effect of Objective Weighting\nL285: ##### PI quality persists: better PI is still better after adding GRPO.\nL286: \nL287: Trajectory PI performs best under vanilla OPSD and remains strongest across most values of $\\lambda$, followed by Skill and Checklist PI. This ordering is consistent through $\\lambda=0.99$ on all benchmarks. Thus, combining OPSD with outcome-based rewards does not eliminate differences in PI quality: stronger PI continues to provide a more effective auxiliary training signal.\nL288: Takeaway. The PI format that performs best under vanilla OPSD generally remains the strongest after adding GRPO, so outcome rewards complement rather than replace effective PI selection.\nL289: ##### GRPO strengthens OPSD.\nL290: Increasing $\\lambda$ generally improves performance for all three PI formats, with the largest gains occurring for the weaker Checklist and Skill formats on ALFWorld and AppWorld. On ALFWorld, several Skill and Trajectory configurations exceed the GRPO-only baseline score, with Trajectory PI reaching $95.13$ at $\\lambda=0.99$ versus the baseline score of $84.96$. On AppWorld, Trajectory PI at $\\lambda=0.99$ reaches $43.90$, exceeding the GRPO score of $42.91$.\nL291: Likewise, on $\\tau^{2}$, it reaches $55.04$, compared with $48.27$ for GRPO.\nL292: Takeaway. Increasing the GRPO weight substantially improves vanilla OPSD. With strong PI and appropriate weighting, the combined objective can outperform a matched GRPO baseline.\nL293: ##### PI improves sample efficiency, but its benefit fades once the policy has learned from it.\nL294: The training curves in Figure cite126†4 show that PI-guided training, particularly with Trajectory PI, improves rapidly during the initial steps. On ALFWorld, all three PI-guided curves plateau or decline after step 100, corresponding to one epoch, while GRPO continues to improve. This suggests that PI provides its largest optimization benefit early in training, before the policy has learned the information it can readily extract from PI.\nL295: Because each training task uses the same static PI throughout training, its supervision may become redundant after the first epoch. Beyond this point, the OPSD signal can become a distraction that interferes with further outcome-based learning. On AppWorld, Trajectory PI reaches nearly $100\\%$ training success at $\\lambda=0.9$ but only $39.14$ test pass@1, suggesting that this improved sample efficiency can also accelerate overfitting on small, less diverse training sets.\nL296: Takeaway. PI accelerates early learning, especially when it contains a strong reference trajectory. Its marginal benefit decreases after the policy has learned the most readily transferable behavior, after which continued OPSD can even hinder further improvement.\nL297: ## 5 Which Offline Metric Should Guide PI Choice?\nL298: We complement the observational analysis of Section cite23†3.2 with two controlled evaluations of the offline PI metrics. ALFWorld-Passcode isolates the effect of leaked information (Section cite34†5.1 ), and per-task selection evaluates whether the KL metrics can identify effective PI from candidate pools (Section cite38†5.2 ); Appendix cite68†E reports additional ablations with corrupted and length-matched PI.\nL299: Together, these studies further demonstrate the limitations of utility and how error-step KL contrast can guide PI selection in practice. Finally, we provide practical recommendations for using the proposed KL metrics. All experiments train Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 with vanilla OPSD on ALFWorld and report held-out pass@1, following Section cite16†2.3 .\nL300: ### 5.1 ALFWorld-Passcode: Leaked Information Can Distort PI Utility\nL301: ##### ALFWorld-Passcode.\nL302: We modify ALFWorld so that completing the task no longer ends the episode. Instead, the final observation reveals a task-specific key, and the episode is scored only on the key the agent submits. The verifier therefore checks only the submitted key, not whether the task was completed.\nL303: We build four PIs from the same GLM-5.2 reference trajectories (Action $+$ Obs.): Key only contains just the reveal and the submission of the key; Mismatched trajectory $+$ key precedes this task’s key with the trajectory of a different task of the same type; Trajectory $+$ key uses the task’s own trajectory, ending with the reveal and the submission of its key; and Trajectory $+$ decoy key is the same trajectory with a wrong key in the final submission.\nL304: Utility is measured as in Equation cite127†2 , with the PI in the student’s prompt. Figure cite128†5 reports OPSD performance, utility, and both KL metrics for each PI.\nL305: Figure 5: Each row is one PI built from the same GLM-5.2 trajectories on ALFWorld-Passcode. Utility ranks the PIs by whether they contain the correct key. $D$ and $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ rank them by whether they contain the task’s own trajectory, and so does OPSD performance. Utility is the untrained student’s pass@1 with the PI in its prompt, split into completed tasks (solid) and copied keys submitted without completing the task (hatched).\nL306: Dashed boxes show the same trajectory in standard ALFWorld, and dashed lines mark the student without PI.\nL307: ##### A PI can score near-perfect utility while teaching nothing.\nL308: With only the correct key in its prompt, the student reaches $98.1$ pass@1, yet training on this PI leaves it exactly where it started. Utility credits the PI for helping a policy that sees it; OPSD, however, trains a student that never does. Any benefit that depends on reading the PI, such as copying a key, therefore cannot transfer. Both KL metrics recognize this, assigning Key only the lowest $D$ and $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ among the four PIs.\nL309: ##### The key changes utility; the trajectory changes learning.\nL310: The remaining PIs vary the key and the trajectory one at a time. Changing only the key, from correct to decoy, moves utility by 25 points but leaves training performance, $D$, and $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ essentially unchanged. Changing only the trajectory, from the task’s own to another task’s, does the reverse.\nL311: The mismatched PI still scores higher utility than the task’s own trajectory in standard ALFWorld or with the decoy key, yet it nearly halves training performance to $45.0$ and lowers both KL metrics. Utility responds to the part of the PI the student can only copy, and the KL metrics to the part it can learn from.\nL312: Takeaway. Utility measures whether PI helps the policy that sees it, so information the student can exploit but never learn, such as a leaked answer, can inflate or deflate it arbitrarily. The KL metrics measure the PI’s effect on the student’s own distribution and are not misled.\nL313: ### 5.2 Selecting PI per Task with Offline Metrics\nL314: ##### Setup.\nL315: For each training task we score a pool of candidate PIs with $D$ and $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$, train with a PI drawn from the top three by either metric, and compare against a random draw from the same pool. The four pools vary along two axes, candidate strength and source diversity: 8 strong PI sources (one winning trajectory per source), 8 GLM-5.3-Flash winning trajectories, 8 self wins from the student, and 8 self wins $+$ 8 self losses.\nL316: The strong-PI pools train for one epoch on the full training set, while the smaller self-rollout pools train for four epochs to keep the number of optimizer updates comparable. Figure cite129†6 shows held-out pass@1 throughout training across three or four seeds; Appendix cite72†F details candidate construction, metric scoring, and training procedures.\nL317: Figure 6: Per-task PI selection on ALFWorld with Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507. Each task trains with a PI drawn from its top 3 candidates by $D$ or by $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$, or uniformly from the whole pool; curves show held-out pass@1 at each evaluation step (mean and standard error over three or four seeds). Error-step KL selection helps most when the pool mixes wins and losses, while reverse-KL selection falls below random except on the GLM-5.3-Flash pool.\nL318: ##### Error-step KL contrast is more effective when PI candidates vary in source or strength.\nL319: Selecting by $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ improves on random selection when the candidates come from different sources, and most when the pool mixes winning and failed rollouts. Among the student’s winning rollouts,it leads random selection by $5.1$ points at step 40, but the gap closes by the end of the third epoch as the student learns the corrections the selected PI provides.\nL320: For winning trajectories from a single strong generator, it matches random selection, likely because the candidates vary little in source or strength, leaving less room for selection to help.\nL321: ##### Reverse KL helps only when PI candidates are strong and come from one source.\nL322: \nL323: Selecting by $D$ gives little benefit except when all candidates come from a single strong generator, GLM-5.3-Flash. In the other pools, it performs slightly worse than random selection, and it fails badly when the pool mixes the student’s own winning and failed rollouts. Failed rollouts shift the student’s distribution the most (Appendix cite68†E ), so ranking candidates by $D$ favors misleading PI.\nL324: Takeaway. Select PI by error-step KL contrast when candidates differ in source or strength; its early advantage among successful self-rollouts closes as the student learns the corrections. Reverse KL helps only when PI candidates are strong and come from a single source.\nL325: ### 5.3 Practical Recommendations\nL326: We recommend using the proposed KL metrics, and error-step KL contrast in particular when candidate PI quality is inconsistent, to select PI likely to perform well under vanilla OPSD, then combining it with a strong GRPO signal to accelerate early learning. Once the benefit of PI plateaus, its weight should be reduced or removed to avoid interfering with further outcome-based learning.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the SPOT: Enhancing Speculative Decodingfor Diffusion Language Modelsvia Commit-Order Prediction (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543)\nciteturn41277view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543\",\"lineno\":439}); Total lines: 1284\nL360:   * Rahimi et al. (2026) E. Rahimi, E. Hirshel, R. Himelstein, A. LeVi, A. Mendelson, and C. Baskin Step-Wise Refusal Dynamics in Autoregressive and Diffusion Language Models. External Links: cite228†Link Cited by: cite174†§1 .\nL362:   * Sun et al. (2026) S. Sun, Y. Li, R. Liu, X. Li, H. Zhen, W. Lin, C. Chen, X. Yu, M. Yuan, and C. Ma PSD: Pushing the Pareto Frontier of Diffusion LLMs via Parallel Speculative Decoding. arXiv. Note: arXiv:2605.15609 [cs.CL] External Links: cite232†Link , cite233†Document†dx.doi.org Cited by: cite174†§1 , cite175†§2 .\nL363:   * Wu et al. (2026) C. Wu, H. Zhang, S. Xue, Z. Liu, S. Diao, L. Zhu, P. Luo, S. Han, and E. Xie Fast-dLLM: Training-free Acceleration of Diffusion LLM by Enabling KV Cache and Parallel Decoding. International Conference on Learning Representations 2026, pp. 57027–57051. External Links: cite234†Link†proceedings.iclr.cc Cited by: cite235†§D.7 , cite174†§1 , cite175†§2 , cite225†§5.4 , cite161†Table 3 .\nL364:   * Xu et al. (2026) Y. Xu, Z. Bao, Q. Wang, Z. Zhang, H. Zhang, B. Peng, A. Li, R. Chalamala, and Y. Lu Multi-Token Residual Prediction. arXiv. Note: arXiv:2605.18817 [cs.LG] External Links: cite236†Link , cite237†Document†dx.doi.org Cited by: cite238†§D.6 , cite159†Table 5 , cite239†Table 6 , cite174†§1 , cite170†§1 , cite171†§2 , cite240†§5.2 .\nL365:   * Ye et al. (2025) J. Ye, Z. Xie, L. Zheng, J. Gao, Z. Wu, X. Jiang, Z. Li, and L. Kong Dream 7B: Diffusion Large Language Models. arXiv. Note: arXiv:2508.15487 [cs.CL] External Links: cite241†Link , cite242†Document†dx.doi.org Cited by: cite179†§H.2 , cite169†§H.3 , cite174†§1 , cite175†§2 , cite172†§5.1 , cite225†§5.4 .\nL366: ## Appendix A Formal Setup and Exactness Proofs\nL367: \nL368: This section makes the decoding semantics used in Section cite14†4 explicit and proves Propositions cite153†1 and cite154†2 .\nL369: \nL370: ### A.1 Formal decoding model\nL371: #### States and terminal states.\nL372: \nL373: Recall\nL374: \nL375:  | $$\\mathcal{X}=(\\mathcal{V}\\cup\\{\\textsc{m}\\})^{L},\\qquad\\mathcal{M}(x)=\\{s:x_{s}=\\textsc{m}\\}.$$  |\nL376: \nL377: Define the nonterminal state space\nL378: \nL379:  | $$\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathrm{nt}}=\\{x\\in\\mathcal{X}:\\mathcal{M}(x)\\neq\\varnothing\\}.$$  |\nL380: \nL381: All target transition maps below are defined on $\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathrm{nt}}$.\nL382: \nL383: The completed causal context $C$ is fixed while an active block is decoded. A target forward at state $x$ returns\nL384: \nL385:  | $$p_{\\theta,s}(\\cdot\\mid x,C)$$  |\nL386: for every $s\\in\\mathcal{M}(x)$.\nL387: #### Idealized target evaluation.\nL388: \nL389: The exactness results concern the mathematical target function: evaluating $p_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid x,C)$ as one replica of a batch is assumed to return the same mathematical conditional as evaluating the same $(x,C)$ alone. This separates algorithmic exactness from finite-precision effects caused by physical batch shape.\nL390: #### Position selection.\nL391: \nL392: The target chooses\nL393: \nL394:  | $$s^{\\star}(x)=\\argmax_{s\\in\\mathcal{M}(x)}\\max_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}p_{\\theta,s}(v\\mid x,C).$$  |\nL395: \nL396: All argmax operations use a fixed deterministic tie-breaking rule. In particular, $s^{\\star}(x)$ depends only on the current target state and not on sampling randomness that has not yet been consumed.\nL397: \nL398: For $s\\in\\mathcal{M}(x)$ and $v\\in\\mathcal{V}$, write\nL399: \nL400:  | $$x\\oplus(s,v)$$  |\nL401: \nL402: for the state obtained by replacing $x_{s}=\\textsc{m}$ by $v$.\nL403: #### Greedy transition.\nL404: \nL405: At $T=0$,\nL406: \nL407:  | $$v^{\\star}_{0}(x)=\\argmax_{v}p_{\\theta,s^{\\star}(x)}(v\\mid x,C),$$  |\nL408: \nL409: and therefore\nL410: \nL411:  | $$\\Phi_{0}:\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathrm{nt}}\\rightarrow\\mathcal{X},\\qquad\\Phi_{0}(x)=x\\oplus\\bigl(s^{\\star}(x),v^{\\star}_{0}(x)\\bigr).$$  |\nL412: \nL413: Starting from initial block state $x_{0}$, draft-free greedy decoding is the deterministic orbit\nL414: \nL415:  | $$x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{0}=x_{0},\\qquad x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{j+1}=\\Phi_{0}(x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{j})$$  |\nL416: \nL417: until a terminal state is reached.\nL418: #### Sampling transition.\nL419: \nL420: At $T>0$, the target token law at position $s$ is\nL421: \nL422:  | $$\\pi_{T,s}(v\\mid x,C)=\\frac{p_{\\theta,s}(v\\mid x,C)^{1/T}}{\\sum_{u\\in\\mathcal{V}}p_{\\theta,s}(u\\mid x,C)^{1/T}}.$$  |\nL423: \nL424: Thus standard draft-free target sampling at state $x$ first chooses the deterministic position $s^{\\star}(x)$ and then draws\nL425: \nL426:  | $$V\\sim\\pi_{T,s^{\\star}(x)}(\\cdot\\mid x,C).$$  |\nL427: #### Shared Gumbel representation.\nL428: \nL429: Let $a(s)$ denote the absolute output position corresponding to local block position $s$. Draw\nL430: \nL431:  | $$G_{a,v}\\overset{\\mathrm{iid}}{\\sim}\\mathrm{Gumbel}(0,1)$$  |\nL432: \nL433: for every absolute position $a$ and token $v$, and let\nL434: \nL435:  | $$G=\\{G_{a,v}\\}_{a,v}.$$  |\nL436: \nL437: For fixed $G$, define\nL438: \nL439:  | $$v^{\\star}_{G}(x)=\\argmax_{v}\\left[\\frac{1}{T}\\log p_{\\theta,s^{\\star}(x)}(v\\mid x,C)+G_{a(s^{\\star}(x)),v}\\right]$$  |\nL440: \nL441: and\nL442:  | $$\\Phi_{G}:\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathrm{nt}}\\rightarrow\\mathcal{X},\\qquad\\Phi_{G}(x)=x\\oplus\\bigl(s^{\\star}(x),v^{\\star}_{G}(x)\\bigr).$$  |\nL443: \nL444: For any fixed realization $G=g$, $\\Phi_{g}$ is deterministic.\nL445: #### Draft trees.\nL446: \nL447: At speculative round $t$, a drafter $\\mathcal{D}$ returns a finite rooted tree\nL448: \nL449:  | $$\\mathcal{T}_{t}=(\\mathcal{N}_{t},\\mathcal{E}_{t})$$  |\nL450: \nL451: whose root is the current committed state $x_{t}$. Every edge $(y,z)\\in\\mathcal{E}_{t}$ corresponds to exactly one hypothetical commitment:\nL452: \nL453:  | $$z=y\\oplus(s,v)$$  |\nL454: \nL455: for some $s\\in\\mathcal{M}(y)$ and $v\\in\\mathcal{V}$.\nL456: The exactness arguments make no assumption about how this tree is constructed. In particular, $\\mathcal{D}$ may be learned or hand-designed, may return a chain or a tree, may depend on all previous target outputs, and at $T>0$ may depend on the shared randomness $G$.\nL457: #### State-equality verifier.\nL458: \nL459: Let $\\Phi$ denote $\\Phi_{0}$ for $T=0$ or $\\Phi_{G}$ after fixing the shared randomness for $T>0$. Beginning at the root $y_{0}=x_{t}$, the verifier proceeds recursively.\nL460: \nL461: If $y_{j}$ has a drafted child $z$ such that\nL462: \nL463:  | $$z=\\Phi(y_{j}),$$  |\nL464: the verifier accepts that child and continues from it. If no such child exists, it rejects all remaining descendants, commits $\\Phi(y_{j})$ directly, and terminates the speculative round. If $y_{j}$ is terminal, no further transition is made.\nL465: \nL466: Since $\\Phi(y_{j})$ is a unique one-step extension, at most one child of a node can be accepted. Hence the accepted portion of an arbitrary draft tree is a single path.\nL467: Let $X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}$ denote the complete draft-free trajectory and $X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}$ the complete committed speculative trajectory. A verifier is distribution preserving when\nL468: \nL469:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}\\overset{d}{=}X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}$$  |\nL470: \nL471: for every admissible drafter $\\mathcal{D}$.\nL472: ### A.2 Proof of Proposition cite153†1 L473: ###### Proof.\nL474: \nL475: Fix target $p_{\\theta}$, causal context $C$, initial block state $x_{0}$, and an arbitrary drafter $\\mathcal{D}$. By the fixed deterministic tie-breaking rule, at $T=0$ the target transition\nL476: \nL477:  | $$\\Phi_{0}:\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathrm{nt}}\\rightarrow\\mathcal{X}$$  |\nL478: \nL479: is deterministic and gives a unique successor for every nonterminal state.\nL480: \nL481: Let\nL482: \nL483:  | $$x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{0}=x_{0},\\qquad x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{k+1}=\\Phi_{0}(x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{k})$$  |\nL484: be the draft-free trajectory, and let $x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k}$ denote the state of the speculative decoder after $k$ committed transitions.\nL485: \nL486: We prove by induction on $k$ that\nL487: \nL488:  | $$x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k}=x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{k}$$  |\nL489: \nL490: for every commitment before termination.\nL491: #### Base case.\nL492: \nL493: For $k=0$, both procedures start from the same initial state:\nL494: \nL495:  | $$x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{0}=x_{0}=x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{0}.$$  |\nL496: #### Inductive step.\nL497: \nL498: Assume\nL499: \nL500:  | $$x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k}=x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{k}$$  |\nL501: \nL502: for some nonterminal state. From $x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k}$, the target has the unique successor\nL503: \nL504:  | $$\\Phi_{0}(x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k}).$$  |\nL505: \nL506: If the draft tree contains this state as a child of $x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k}$, state-equality verification accepts that child. Otherwise, the verifier rejects the speculative continuation and commits the same state as the target fallback. Hence, in either case,\nL507:  | $\\displaystyle x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k+1}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\Phi_{0}(x^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{k})$  |\nL508:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\Phi_{0}(x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{k})$  |\nL509:  |  | $\\displaystyle=x^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{k+1}.$  |\nL510: \nL511: Thus the induction hypothesis holds for $k+1$.\nL512: \nL513: Since $\\Phi_{0}(x)$ is unique, at most one child of any tree node can be accepted. Therefore, even for an arbitrary finite draft tree, the accepted states form a single path, and the argument above applies to every committed transition.\nL514: \nL515: By induction,\nL516:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{0,\\mathcal{D}}=X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{0}.$$  |\nL517: \nL518: ∎\nL519: \nL520: The argument is block-local only for notation. Since the completed block is identical to draft-free decoding, the causal context of the next block is also identical. Induction over blocks therefore gives equality of the complete generated sequence at $T=0$.\nL521: ### A.3 Proof of Proposition cite154†2 L522: ###### Proof.\nL523: \nL524: Fix $T>0$, target $p_{\\theta}$, causal context $C$, initial state $x_{0}$, and an arbitrary drafter $\\mathcal{D}$.\nL525: \nL526: The proof has three steps. First, we show that the shared-Gumbel construction induces the same draft-free trajectory distribution as ordinary temperature-$T$ sampling. Second, conditioned on a realization of the Gumbel table, verification reduces to the deterministic case of Proposition cite153†1 . Third, we marginalize over the shared randomness.\nL527: #### Step 1: Gumbel-driven draft-free law.\nL528: \nL529: Fix a nonterminal state $x$ and let\nL530: \nL531:  | $$s=s^{\\star}(x).$$  |\nL532: \nL533: Since $\\{G_{a(s),v}\\}_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}$ are independent standard Gumbels, the Gumbel–argmax identity gives\nL534:  | $\\displaystyle\\Pr[v^{\\star}_{G}(x)=u]$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\exp\\!\\left(T^{-1}\\log p_{\\theta,s}(u\\mid x,C)\\right)}{\\sum_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}\\exp\\!\\left(T^{-1}\\log p_{\\theta,s}(v\\mid x,C)\\right)}$  |  | (5)\nL535:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{p_{\\theta,s}(u\\mid x,C)^{1/T}}{\\sum_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}p_{\\theta,s}(v\\mid x,C)^{1/T}}$  |  | (6)\nL536:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\pi_{T,s}(u\\mid x,C).$  |  | (7)\nL537: \nL538: Thus, for any fixed state, the Gumbel construction induces exactly the temperature-$T$ target token law.\nL539: It remains to show that using a single Gumbel table indexed by absolute position preserves this law along the full adaptive trajectory. Consider commitment step $j$ of Gumbel-driven draft-free decoding, with current state $x_{j}$ and selected position\nL540: \nL541:  | $$s_{j}=s^{\\star}(x_{j}).$$  |\nL542: Let $\\mathcal{F}_{j}$ denote the history generated by the states and Gumbel rows used during the first $j$ commitments. Since each commitment fills a previously masked position, $s_{j}$ has not been committed before, and therefore the row\nL543: \nL544:  | $$G_{a(s_{j}),\\cdot}$$  |\nL545: \nL546: has not previously been used.\nL547: Moreover, $s_{j}$ is determined by $x_{j}$ and hence by $\\mathcal{F}_{j}$; its selection does not depend on any unused Gumbel row. Since the Gumbel rows are independent across absolute positions, $G_{a(s_{j}),\\cdot}$ remains an independent standard-Gumbel vector conditional on $\\mathcal{F}_{j}$. Therefore,\nL548: \nL549:  | $$\\Pr\\!\\left[v^{\\star}_{G}(x_{j})=u\\mid\\mathcal{F}_{j}\\right]=\\pi_{T,s_{j}}(u\\mid x_{j},C).$$  |\nL550: Hence the Gumbel-driven decoder has the same conditional transition law as ordinary draft-free sampling at every commitment. Since both start from $x_{0}$, the chain rule gives\nL551: \nL552:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(G)\\overset{d}{=}X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}.$$  |  | (8)\nL553: #### Step 2: conditional pathwise exactness.\nL554: \nL555: Fix any realization $G=g$. Then\nL556: \nL557:  | $$\\Phi_{g}:\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathrm{nt}}\\rightarrow\\mathcal{X}$$  |\nL558: \nL559: is a deterministic transition map.\nL560: \nL561: The proof of Proposition cite153†1 requires only that the target have a unique deterministic successor at each state and that a drafted child be accepted exactly when it equals that successor. Both properties hold with $\\Phi_{g}$ in place of $\\Phi_{0}$. Therefore, for every realization $g$,\nL562:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}(g)=X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(g).$$  |  | (9)\nL563: \nL564: This argument is independent of how $\\mathcal{D}$ constructs its draft. In particular, the draft may depend on stale target distributions, learned parameters, previous decoding history, or the shared table $G$ itself; a drafted edge is committed only when it equals the target successor under the same realization $g$.\nL565: #### Step 3: marginalization.\nL566: \nL567: By Step 2, for every realization $g$ of the shared Gumbel table,\nL568: \nL569:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}(g)=X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(g).$$  |\nL570: \nL571: Therefore, when $G$ is drawn according to its Gumbel distribution,\nL572: \nL573:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}(G)=X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(G).$$  |\nL574: \nL575: By definition, $X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}$ denotes this same random speculative trajectory with its dependence on $G$ left implicit.\nL576: \nL577: Hence, for any set $A$ of complete decoding trajectories,\nL578:  | $$\\Pr\\!\\left[X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}\\in A\\right]=\\Pr\\!\\left[X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(G)\\in A\\right].$$  |\nL579: \nL580: Using equation cite243†8 ,\nL581: \nL582:  | $$\\Pr\\!\\left[X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}\\in A\\right]=\\Pr\\!\\left[X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}\\in A\\right].$$  |\nL583: \nL584: Since this holds for every $A$,\nL585: \nL586:  | $$X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}\\overset{d}{=}X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}.$$  |\n"
